{{Politics of Okaiken}}
The {{Nihongo|'''60th general election of members of the House of Commoners'''|第60回衆議院議員総選挙|dai-rokujūkai Shūgiin giin sōsenkyo}} was held on 27 October 2025, four years after the previous election. Voting took place in all [[List of districts of the House of Commoners of Japan|Representatives constituencies]] of [[Okaiken]] to appoint [[List of members of the Diet of Okaiken|Members of Diet]] to all 475 seats in the [[House of Commoners of Okaiken|House of Commoners]].

The election resulted in a significant realignment of the Okaiken political landscape. The incumbent [[Progressive Party of Okaiken|Progressive Party (PPO)]] of Prime Minister [[Honda Shigetsuma]] lost 20 seats but remained the largest party. The [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Liberal Democratic Party (LDP)]], under new leader [[Koichiro Sadanuma]], saw a major resurgence, gaining 40 seats to become the clear second-largest party and Leader of the Opposition.

The biggest story of the night was the historic comeback of the [[Okaiken Socialist Party|Okaiken Socialist Party (OSP)]], which went from 2 seats to 50 under its new 32-year-old leader, [[Rento Nakayama]]. Conversely, the [[Sanese Communist Party|Sanese Communist Party (SCP)]] collapsed, losing half its seats and its leader, [[Shintaro Hamabe]], who resigned.

Lacking a majority, Prime Minister Honda entered into coalition talks with Nakayama, resulting in a PPO-OSP coalition government.

==Background==
The 59th Diet was led by [[Honda Shigetsuma]]'s [[Progressive Party of Okaiken|PPO]] as a minority government. The four-year term was defined by relative economic stability and steady growth, which the PPO championed as its main achievement. However, the period was also marked by contentious political battles, chiefly the passage of the [[National Universal Health Act]]. While popular with the public, its massive cost and subsequent tax increases became the primary attack line for the opposition.

The PPO government also enacted several liberal immigration reforms to address labor shortages, a move that proved divisive and was criticized by both the LDP and the [[Social Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Social Democratic Party (SDP)]].

All major opposition parties entered the election with new leadership. The LDP, after a bruising internal battle, bypassed its far-right faction led by Hakuya Terakubo and elected 44-year-old moderate [[Koichiro Sadanuma]] as its new president. The SCP, following its 2021 defeat, elected the more ideological [[Shintaro Hamabe]] to replace [[Sho Aoyagi]]. Most notably, the near-extinct OSP held a leadership election that was won by [[Rento Nakayama]], a 32-year-old former actor and fitness influencer who ran a campaign almost entirely on social media.

==Election campaign==
The campaign was defined by three major narratives: the PPO's defense of its economic record, the LDP's resurgence as a fiscally conservative alternative, and the explosive rise of "Nakayama-mania."

===PPO: The "Smug" Incumbent===
The PPO, led by the still-popular Honda Shigetsuma, ran on a simple platform of "Keep the Growth Going." Their campaign was well-funded and professional, but was criticized by the media as "safe" and "uninspired."

A significant gaffe occurred during the first KTK leaders' debate. When pressed by LDP leader Sadanuma on the inflation caused by the Universal Health Act, Honda appeared dismissive. "The LDP complains about a 2% rise in prices," Honda said, "but they are silent on the 10% rise in prosperity my government has delivered. It is a cost we can afford." The clip was immediately seized by the LDP and OSP, who ran it in attack ads juxtaposed with prices of groceries, painting Honda as "smug" and "out of touch" with the daily struggles of citizens.

===LDP: The Resurgent Opposition===
Under the new leadership of 44-year-old Koichiro Sadanuma, the LDP presented a fresh, unified, and energetic front. Sadanuma, a center-right conservative, successfully mended the party's internal factions and focused relentlessly on two issues: the "unsustainable" cost of the PPO's [[National Universal Health Act]] and the "threat to social cohesion" from the PPO's immigration policies.

The party's campaign was briefly rocked by a controversy when Hakuya Terakubo, the passed-over far-right candidate, gave an unsanctioned speech at a rally where he stated, "We must protect the purity of Okaiken and ensure Okaiken is for the Sanese." Sadanuma faced intense criticism for waiting 48 hours to condemn the remarks, which the SCP and PPO claimed was proof of a "hidden radical agenda" within the LDP.

===OSP: The "Nakayama-mania" Phenomenon===
The most dynamic element of the campaign was the "Nakayama-mania" surrounding new OSP leader [[Rento Nakayama]]. The 32-year-old former actor and bodybuilder bypassed traditional media, running a viral campaign on TikTok and Instagram. His rallies, which often began with him doing push-ups on stage, attracted thousands of young, previously disengaged voters.

Nakayama's platform was a unique mix of left-leaning economics and populist grievance. He attacked "hidden inflation" on groceries, advocated for massive state investment in mental health and preventative care, and criticized the university system for producing "too many useless degrees and not enough scientists and plumbers."

His campaign was a media sensation, but his inexperience was exposed in the final leaders' debate. When asked a basic foreign policy question about the ongoing Ameka Islands dispute, Nakayama was unable to name Okaiken's Minister of Foreign Affairs, drawing ridicule from his opponents. While the gaffe caused a minor dip in the polls, his energized base remained loyal, seeing it as proof he was an "outsider," not a "political elite."

===SCP: The "Parasite" Gaffe and Collapse===
The [[Sanese Communist Party|SCP]], under its new hard-line leader Shintaro Hamabe, attempted to win back its base by running on a platform of "A True Left Alternative." Hamabe called for the nationalization of energy companies and a new "Parasite Tax" on individuals with a net worth over 500 million En.

The campaign imploded on October 10. While touring a shopping district, Hamabe was confronted by a small hardware store owner about the proposed tax. A news camera filmed Hamabe becoming irate, jabbing his finger at the man and shouting, "It is men like you, capitalist parasites, who are bleeding this country dry!" The video went viral. The LDP and PPO both ran ads simply showing the clip, followed by the text: "This is what the Communist Party truly thinks of you." Polling for the SCP, already low, fell off a cliff.

