The election night confirmed the polling trends. The [[Progressive Party of Okaiken|PPO]] suffered a modest but disappointing loss of 20 seats, finishing with 181, well short of a majority. Prime Minister Honda called the result "a message of dissatisfaction, which I have heard clearly."

The [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|LDP]] saw its resurgence confirmed, gaining 40 seats to land at 128, firmly establishing it as the sole party of the conservative opposition. New leader Koichiro Sadanuma called the result a "clear mandate to fight for fiscal sanity in the Diet."

The most dramatic result was the "Nakayama-mania" of the [[Okaiken Socialist Party|OSP]]. The party, which held only 2 seats, rocketed to 50 seats, a historic gain of +48, making it the third-largest party and the new "kingmaker" in the Diet.

The [[Sanese Communist Party|SCP]] collapsed, losing half its seats to fall from 90 to 45. The loss was exacerbated by SCP leader [[Shintaro Hamabe]] losing his own seat. In a brief statement, Hamabe took full responsibility for the "catastrophic" result and announced his immediate resignation as party chairman.

The smaller parties were squeezed by the LDP and OSP. The [[New Komeito Party|NKP]] and [[Social Democratic Party (Okaiken)|SDP]] both saw significant losses, while the [[Okaiken Innovation Party|OIP]] saw a modest gain of 4 seats.

===Notable losses===
*'''[[Shintaro Hamabe]]''' (SCP), the leader of the Sanese Communist Party, lost his own seat in Seto-3rd district, capping the party's disastrous night.

===Notable wins===
*'''[[Rento Nakayama]]''' (OSP), the new leader of the Socialist Party, won his Okami-3rd district with 62% of the vote.
*'''[[Yoshida Shimane]]''' (SDP), leader of the Social Democrats, successfully defended the Miyagi-1st district seat she had won in a 2022 by-election.
*All other major party leaders—[[Honda Shigetsuma]] (PPO), [[Koichiro Sadanuma]] (LDP), [[Soichiro Onizuka]] (NKP), and [[Kei Yamada]] (OIP)—successfully defended their respective seats.

==Aftermath==
With no party holding the 238 seats needed for a majority, the PPO (181) immediately entered into negotiations with the OSP (50). While the PPO was more centrist than the OSP, their platforms were seen as compatible. On November 4, 2025, the two parties announced a formal coalition agreement. [[Honda Shigetsuma]] would remain Prime Minister, while [[Rento Nakayama]] would join the cabinet as Minister of Health and Welfare, a key position allowing him to pursue his campaign's signature issues. The new PPO-OSP coalition government held 231 seats, just short of a majority, requiring them to seek cooperation from other minor parties on a case-by-case basis.
